# =============================================================================
# Build context exclusions for docker/api.Dockerfile
# =============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING. Without it the image was silently broken, and it
# is worth writing down exactly how, because it failed in a way that looked
# like success for weeks.
#
# The Dockerfile does:
#
#     COPY --from=deps --chown=node:node /app/node_modules ./node_modules
#     COPY --chown=node:node . .
#
# The second COPY brings in the whole build context. With no .dockerignore that
# includes the DEVELOPER'S OWN node_modules, which Docker merges on top of the
# tree the deps stage just built - overwriting any file present in both.
#
# So the published image did not contain the dependencies the lockfile
# describes. It contained the developer's, layered over them. Consequences, in
# ascending order of seriousness:
#
#   1. The image is not reproducible. Two people building the same commit get
#      different images, and neither matches what CI builds.
#   2. Native binaries for the wrong platform get baked into a Linux image.
#      Prisma's query engine is one file per platform
#      (query_engine-windows.dll.node vs libquery_engine-debian-*.so.node), so
#      building on Windows shipped both and it happened to keep working.
#   3. It hid a real break until it did not. `npm ci` on the host replaces the
#      GENERATED Prisma client with the ungenerated stub, so the stub
#      overwrote the client the deps stage had generated and the container
#      started answering 503 with "@prisma/client did not initialize yet".
#      That is what surfaced this.
#
# The build only ever worked because the host happened to have run
# `prisma generate`. That is not a property of the repository, it is a property
# of one machine.
# =============================================================================

# The reason this file exists. Dependencies come from the deps stage, which
# installs them from the lockfile and runs `prisma generate` against a Linux
# target.
node_modules

# Secrets. There is no Backend/.env today, but the whole point of an ignore
# file is that it holds when someone creates one.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and editor state - large, and meaningless inside an image.
.git
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea

# Platform config for the deployment this migration is leaving.
.vercelignore
vercel.json

# Build and test debris.
npm-debug.log*
*.log
coverage
.nyc_output

# Runtime upload target. The image creates it and the compose volume mounts
# over it; anything committed here would be shadowed anyway.
uploads

# Docker's own files, when built from the repository root rather than ./Backend.
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
